From 79444dcc6847fc42e8ceb7a2c339442c7b75a3c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 14:50:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] hostapd: let a preserved BSS keep the address it already has

iface_config_macaddr_list() marks every address of the running configuration
of a PHY, and every MLD address, with -1. The branch that carries a preserved
BSS's address into the new configuration read that as taken and was therefore
never reached, so the address stayed unclaimed in macaddr_list while the BSS
went on using it. The generator of step 6 then handed the same address to a
newly added BSS:

    Duplicate BSSID b6:ad:9f:70:fb:4a on interface 'ap-mld6' and 'ap-mld0'.
    Failed to add new bss ap-mld0 on phy phy0.0
    Restart interface for phy phy0.0

hostapd refused its own configuration and restarted the PHY under the stations
it served. A four address backhaul station survived that restart as associated
and stopped carrying data, which took a Multi-AP agent off the mesh until its
daemon was restarted.

The address is asked about by the BSS that already holds it, so a reservation
over it is its own. Measured on a seven BSS AP MLD: the reconfiguration that
produced the three lines above now produces none of them, and the backhaul
station keeps its association across it.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
(cherry picked from commit 79444dcc6847fc42e8ceb7a2c339442c7b75a3c3)
---
 package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc b/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc
index 218cad4b..cdc41ce3 100644
--- a/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc
+++ b/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc
@@ -822,11 +822,14 @@ function iface_reload_config(name, phydev, config, old_config)
 			return false;
 
 		// try to preserve MAC address of this BSS by reassigning another
-		// BSS if necessary
+		// BSS if necessary. iface_config_macaddr_list() marks addresses of
+		// the running config with -1, so only a claim by a different BSS
+		// blocks reuse here.
+		let held = macaddr_list[prev_config.bssid];
 		if ((cur_config.default_macaddr || cur_config.random_macaddr) &&
 		    cur_config.random_macaddr == prev_config.random_macaddr &&
 		    cur_config.default_macaddr == prev_config.default_macaddr &&
-		    !macaddr_list[prev_config.bssid]) {
+		    (held == null || held == -1 || held == i)) {
 			macaddr_list[prev_config.bssid] = i;
 			cur_config.bssid = prev_config.bssid;
 		}
From 4c6992b13bfab5105ea1f91f57e5c23601cae1c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 20:34:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] hostapd: keep MLD interfaces and their BSSes across config
 changes

A config change below MLD level destroyed and recreated MLD netdevs and BSSes
that could have been kept. This restarted the PHY and dropped all connected
stations. Three separate issues caused this:

1. mld_find_matching_config() compared the whole vif config, so a change to an
   SSID created a new MLD. mld_set_config() then reloaded every interface
   without it, bss_check_mld() found no registry entry, iface_check_mld()
   spliced all MLD BSSes out of the config, and the interface was removed when
   no BSS was left. Replace it with mld_config_matches(), which compares only
   the fields that define the netdev: name, wiphy, radio mask and address.
   All other fields are BSS level and reach hostapd through the per PHY config
   file. The radio mask is compared instead of the radio list, so a new order
   of the device list keeps the MLD. mld_rename_bss() has no callers left and
   is removed.

2. bss_find_existing() accepted a hash match between MLD BSSes with different
   names, which happens when the BSS set shifts and BSSes share the same
   settings. Step 4 of iface_reload_config() then renamed one MLD netdev onto
   the name of another one, which fails with EEXIST. An MLD BSS is identified
   by its netdev name, so skip old MLD BSSes with a different name. The search
   continues, and an old BSS with the correct name is kept.

3. Step 2 preserved and renamed the first BSS even when the first BSS changes
   between plain BSS and MLD link. This cannot work, because
   hostapd_bss_setup_multi_link() only runs when a BSS is allocated. A rename
   of an MLD link also renames the netdev that the other radios share. Delete
   and recreate the first BSS instead. If no other BSS is kept, keep the old
   first BSS until the first new BSS exists, so that the PHY always has a BSS.
   Its address stays reserved until the delete. Delete it right after that,
   because its netdev and /var/run/hostapd/<name> keep its name, and a later
   new BSS can use that name. If another BSS is kept, delete the old first BSS
   in step 3 as before, since a kept BSS can be renamed onto its name.
   bss_remove() holds the delete sequence for both paths.

Tested on an AP MLD with 7 BSSes, going from 1 to 7 configured BSSes. The
messages "Skip MLD interface", "Remove interface", "Restart interface for phy"
and "Renaming device ... failed: Object exists" no longer appear, and a
4-address backhaul station stays associated across the change.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
(cherry picked from commit 4c6992b13bfab5105ea1f91f57e5c23601cae1c3)
---
 .../network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc | 107 +++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 77 insertions(+), 30 deletions(-)

diff --git a/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc b/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc
index cdc41ce3..41a01951 100644
--- a/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc
+++ b/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc
@@ -574,6 +574,12 @@ function bss_find_existing(config, prev_config, prev_hash)
 		if (!prev_hash[i] || hash != prev_hash[i])
 			continue;
 
+		// An MLD BSS is identified by its netdev name, which is shared with
+		// all radios that contribute a link. A rename onto the name of
+		// another MLD fails with EEXIST.
+		if (config.mld_ap && prev_config.bss[i].ifname != config.ifname)
+			continue;
+
 		prev_hash[i] = null;
 		return i;
 	}
@@ -603,6 +609,14 @@ function get_config_bss(name, config, idx)
 	return if_bss[ifname];
 }
 
+function bss_remove(name, bss, config)
+{
+	hostapd.printf(`Remove bss '${config.ifname}' on phy '${name}'`);
+	bss.delete();
+	if (!config.mld_ap)
+		wdev_remove(config.ifname);
+}
+
 const radio_chan_fields = [
 	"op_class",
 	"vht_oper_chwidth", "vht_oper_centr_freq_seg0_idx",
@@ -843,8 +857,21 @@ function iface_reload_config(name, phydev, config, old_config)
 		return false;
 	}
 
+	// A BSS cannot change between plain and MLD link in place, because
+	// hostapd_bss_setup_multi_link() only runs when a BSS is allocated. A
+	// rename to an MLD name fails as well, since bss_check_mld() already
+	// created that netdev, and a rename of an MLD link renames the netdev of
+	// all radios. Delete and recreate the first BSS instead. If no other BSS
+	// is kept, keep the old one until the first new BSS exists, so that the
+	// PHY never runs without a BSS, but no longer: its netdev and its
+	// control socket keep its name, which a later new BSS can reuse.
+	let deferred_bss, deferred_cfg;
+	const first_bss_converts =
+		(config.bss[0].mld_ap || old_config.bss[0].mld_ap) &&
+		old_config.bss[0].ifname != config.bss[0].ifname;
+
 	// Step 2: if none were found, rename and preserve the first one
-	if (length(bss_list) == 0) {
+	if (length(bss_list) == 0 && !first_bss_converts) {
 		// can't change the bssid of the first bss
 		if (config.bss[0].bssid != old_config.bss[0].bssid) {
 			if (!config.bss[0].default_macaddr) {
@@ -874,11 +901,17 @@ function iface_reload_config(name, phydev, config, old_config)
 		if (!prev_bss)
 			return false;
 
-		let ifname = old_config.bss[i].ifname;
-		hostapd.printf(`Remove bss '${ifname}' on phy '${name}'`);
-		prev_bss.delete();
-		if (!old_config.bss[i].mld_ap)
-			wdev_remove(ifname);
+		if (!i && !length(bss_list)) {
+			deferred_bss = prev_bss;
+			deferred_cfg = old_config.bss[0];
+			// The address is still in use until the deferred delete, but it
+			// must not be assigned to any of the new BSSes.
+			if (deferred_cfg.bssid)
+				macaddr_list[deferred_cfg.bssid] = -1;
+			continue;
+		}
+
+		bss_remove(name, prev_bss, old_config.bss[i]);
 	}
 
 	// Step 4: rename preserved interfaces, use temporary name on duplicates
@@ -963,6 +996,11 @@ function iface_reload_config(name, phydev, config, old_config)
 			hostapd.printf(`Failed to add new bss ${ifname} on phy ${name}`);
 			return false;
 		}
+
+		if (deferred_bss) {
+			bss_remove(name, deferred_bss, deferred_cfg);
+			deferred_bss = null;
+		}
 	}
 
 	// Step 8: update interface bss order
@@ -1258,13 +1296,14 @@ function bss_config(bss_name) {
 	}
 }
 
-function mld_rename_bss(data, name)
+function mld_radio_mask(radios)
 {
-	if (data.ifname == name)
-		return true;
+	let radio_mask = 0;
+	for (let r in radios)
+		if (r != null)
+			radio_mask |= 1 << r;
 
-	// TODO: handle rename gracefully
-	return false;
+	return radio_mask;
 }
 
 function mld_add_bss(name, data, phy_list, i)
@@ -1292,20 +1331,30 @@ function mld_add_bss(name, data, phy_list, i)
 		data.default_macaddr = true;
 	}
 
-	let radio_mask = 0;
-	for (let r in config.radios)
-		if (r != null)
-			radio_mask |= 1 << r;
-
-	data.radio_mask = radio_mask;
+	data.radio_mask = mld_radio_mask(config.radios);
 	data.ifname = name;
 }
 
-function mld_find_matching_config(list, config)
+// An MLD netdev is defined by its name, wiphy, radio mask and address, i.e. the
+// config fields used by mld_add_bss(). The caller looks up the previous entry by
+// name. All remaining fields are BSS level and reach hostapd through the per PHY
+// config file, so a change to them must not destroy the netdev and all BSSes
+// attached to it.
+function mld_config_matches(data, config)
 {
-	for (let name, data in list)
-		if (is_equal(data.config, config))
-			return name;
+	if (!data.ifname || !data.config)
+		return false;
+
+	if (data.config.phy != config.phy)
+		return false;
+
+	if (data.radio_mask != mld_radio_mask(config.radios))
+		return false;
+
+	if (config.macaddr)
+		return data.macaddr == config.macaddr;
+
+	return !!data.default_macaddr;
 }
 
 function mld_reload_interface(name)
@@ -1329,16 +1378,14 @@ function mld_set_config(config)
 
 	hostapd.printf(`Set MLD config: ${keys(config)}`);
 
-	// find renamed/new interfaces
+	// find kept/new interfaces
 	for (let name, data in config) {
-		let prev = mld_find_matching_config(prev_mld, data);
-		if (prev) {
-			let data = prev_mld[prev];
-			if (mld_rename_bss(data, name)) {
-				new_mld[name] = data;
-				delete prev_mld[prev];
-				continue;
-			}
+		let prev = prev_mld[name];
+		if (prev && mld_config_matches(prev, data)) {
+			prev.config = data;
+			new_mld[name] = prev;
+			delete prev_mld[name];
+			continue;
 		}
 
 		new_mld[name] = {
From 87623c1e67a95d359da65be51ec4f2af6354ecec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 12:25:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] hostapd: give the radio 0 link of an MLD the MLD address

bss_macaddr_next() gives the address of an MLD to its link on radio 0, and
the address generation relies on that. Two paths keep an old link address
instead, even after the MLD address changed:

- Step 2 of iface_reload_config() keeps the old BSSID of the first BSS when
  the new one is a default address, because the first BSS cannot change it
  in place.
- The keep loop in iface_restart() gives a surviving BSS with a default
  address its previous BSSID again.

The link then keeps its old address, and the MLD address is on no link.

In one case, a config change gave ap-mld0 a new MLD address and reloaded it
in place. Link 0 kept the previous MLD address as its link address, so the
new MLD address was on no link. From then on, about 30 % of the unicast
frames to the associated backhaul station were lost (68 of 100 echo requests
arrived), while the AP counted no failed transmission. A new association did
not clear it; a full wifi down/up did.

Move the decision of bss_macaddr_next() into bss_mld_addr(). The keep loop
of iface_restart() skips a link whose previous address is not the MLD
address, so the link gets the MLD address. Step 2 takes the restart path in
the same case. A link that already has the MLD address stays in place, and
plain BSSes, links on other radios and static addresses keep the old
behaviour.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
(cherry picked from commit 87623c1e67a95d359da65be51ec4f2af6354ecec)
---
 .../network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc | 23 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc b/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc
index 41a01951..e92425a6 100644
--- a/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc
+++ b/package/network/services/hostapd/files/hostapd.uc
@@ -352,13 +352,18 @@ function iface_macaddr_init(phydev, config, macaddr_list)
 // mld_add_bss() takes it from radio 0, so only a link on radio 0 can use it. A
 // link on another radio must keep the reservation, because the two links would
 // otherwise use one address.
-function bss_macaddr_next(phydev, macaddr_list, bss, idx)
+function bss_mld_addr(phydev, macaddr_list, bss)
 {
 	let mld_addr = bss.mld_bssid;
 	if (phydev.radio || macaddr_list[mld_addr] != -1)
-		mld_addr = null;
+		return null;
+
+	return mld_addr;
+}
 
-	return phydev.macaddr_next(idx, mld_addr);
+function bss_macaddr_next(phydev, macaddr_list, bss, idx)
+{
+	return phydev.macaddr_next(idx, bss_mld_addr(phydev, macaddr_list, bss));
 }
 
 function csa_timer_cancel(name)
@@ -404,6 +409,7 @@ function iface_restart(phydev, config, old_config)
 	// compares the link addresses in the association response with the
 	// addresses it learned. If the addresses differ, the non-AP MLD drops the
 	// association. Therefore a BSS that survives the restart keeps its address.
+	// A link that must carry the MLD address follows the MLD address instead.
 	for (let i = 0; i < length(config.bss); i++) {
 		let bss = config.bss[i];
 		let prev = prev_bssid[bss.ifname];
@@ -411,6 +417,10 @@ function iface_restart(phydev, config, old_config)
 		if (!bss.default_macaddr || !prev || macaddr_list[prev] != null)
 			continue;
 
+		let mld_addr = bss_mld_addr(phydev, macaddr_list, bss);
+		if (mld_addr && prev != mld_addr)
+			continue;
+
 		bss.bssid = prev;
 		macaddr_list[prev] = i;
 		keep[i] = true;
@@ -873,6 +883,13 @@ function iface_reload_config(name, phydev, config, old_config)
 	// Step 2: if none were found, rename and preserve the first one
 	if (length(bss_list) == 0 && !first_bss_converts) {
 		// can't change the bssid of the first bss
+		let mld_addr = config.bss[0].default_macaddr &&
+			bss_mld_addr(phydev, macaddr_list, config.bss[0]);
+		if (mld_addr && old_config.bss[0].bssid != mld_addr) {
+			hostapd.printf(`MLD address of first interface changed: ${lc(old_config.bss[0].bssid)} -> ${lc(mld_addr)}`);
+			return false;
+		}
+
 		if (config.bss[0].bssid != old_config.bss[0].bssid) {
 			if (!config.bss[0].default_macaddr) {
 				hostapd.printf(`BSSID of first interface changed: ${lc(old_config.bss[0].bssid)} -> ${lc(config.bss[0].bssid)}`);
From 9a81bf528dcd3e6d868492ab293263d186336992 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 14:47:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] hostapd: move the control socket with a renamed BSS

With CONFIG_IEEE80211BE, hostapd names the control socket of a BSS
after hapd->ctrl_sock_iface, not after conf->iface. bss.rename()
changed conf->iface and opened the socket again, but kept the old
ctrl_sock_iface. The renamed BSS therefore kept the socket of its old
name.

A reload that renames phy0-ap0 to phy0-ap2 and then adds a new
phy0-ap0 failed for this reason. The new BSS found
/var/run/hostapd/phy0-ap0 bound by the renamed BSS ("ctrl_iface exists
and seems to be in use"), add_bss failed, and hostapd.uc restarted
the whole phy. Measured on slot-1-agent2, 2026-09-28 13:56:19 (phy0)
and 13:56:21 (phy1): each restart took all BSSes of the phy down for
about 2 s.

Set ctrl_sock_iface from the new name before the socket is opened
again, as start_ctrl_iface_bss() does for a new BSS.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
(cherry picked from commit 9a81bf528dcd3e6d868492ab293263d186336992)
---
 package/network/services/hostapd/src/src/ap/ucode.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/package/network/services/hostapd/src/src/ap/ucode.c b/package/network/services/hostapd/src/src/ap/ucode.c
index 2e26757e..04d2bf65 100644
--- a/package/network/services/hostapd/src/src/ap/ucode.c
+++ b/package/network/services/hostapd/src/src/ap/ucode.c
@@ -832,6 +832,7 @@ uc_hostapd_bss_rename(uc_vm_t *vm, size_t nargs)
 	if (!strncmp(hapd->conf->ssid.vlan, hapd->conf->iface, sizeof(hapd->conf->ssid.vlan)))
 		os_strlcpy(hapd->conf->ssid.vlan, ifname, sizeof(hapd->conf->ssid.vlan));
 	os_strlcpy(hapd->conf->iface, ifname, sizeof(hapd->conf->iface));
+	hostapd_set_ctrl_sock_iface(hapd);
 	hostapd_ubus_add_bss(hapd);
 
 	hostapd_ucode_update_interfaces();
From b833ad3511a67099dd3d0f0b98f4cea5f009dd69 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 14:47:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] hostapd: initialise the result of bss.rename()

bss.rename(ifname, true) skips the driver rename, and ret was then
read without a value. hostapd.uc passes no second argument today, so
no caller reaches this.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
(cherry picked from commit b833ad3511a67099dd3d0f0b98f4cea5f009dd69)
---
 package/network/services/hostapd/src/src/ap/ucode.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/package/network/services/hostapd/src/src/ap/ucode.c b/package/network/services/hostapd/src/src/ap/ucode.c
index 04d2bf65..20d3d5c6 100644
--- a/package/network/services/hostapd/src/src/ap/ucode.c
+++ b/package/network/services/hostapd/src/src/ap/ucode.c
@@ -800,7 +800,7 @@ uc_hostapd_bss_rename(uc_vm_t *vm, size_t nargs)
 	char prev_ifname[IFNAMSIZ + 1];
 	struct sta_info *sta;
 	const char *ifname;
-	int ret;
+	int ret = 0;
 
 	if (!hapd || ucv_type(ifname_arg) != UC_STRING)
 		return NULL;
